# Localization

- Repo-root paths are the canonical English source. Each target locale (today only `es`) mirrors them under `<locale>/` with identical relative paths and filenames. Locales come from `docs.json` `navigation.languages`; there is no separate i18n config.
- Translations are written by hand (or by an agent) in the same PR as the English change. There is no translation service or post-merge translation workflow. Every PR that adds, changes, moves or deletes an English page makes the matching change to `es/**` too, including the Spanish navigation in `docs.json`.
- Use `i18n/glossary.md` for voice and fixed term translations. When you settle on a new term, add it there in the same PR.
- Small English edits that don't change meaning (typo, formatting) don't need a Spanish change; anything that changes what a reader learns does.
- Run `npm run check` before opening a PR. CI (`.github/workflows/validate-docs.yml`) runs the same checks.
- Keep all locale navigation in `docs.json` under `navigation.languages`; do not reintroduce `navigation.global` or top-level `navbar`.
- Keep SEO metatags in English unless explicitly requested otherwise.
- Do not translate code snippets, inline code, URLs, route slugs, or API identifiers.
- Shared media lives under repo-root `assets/`. Locale pages sit one directory deeper than their English page, so relative `src` paths need one more `../` per locale segment. `npm run translate:localize-mdx-paths` fixes `assets/` `src` depth, `snippets/` import depth and `<locale>/components/*.jsx` imports; `npm run translate:localize-links` rewrites markdown `](/...)` and JSX `href="/..."` absolute links to the locale prefix. Both have `:check` variants in CI.
- Shared MDX snippets live under repo-root `snippets/` (not per-locale) and stay English. Import them with relative paths from each page.
- Locale-aware React components that can contain text live under `components/*.jsx` for English and `<locale>/components/*.jsx` for targets, and are maintained by hand per locale (do not keep these in shared `snippets/`).
- Endpoint and webhook MDX must pin a **repo-local** OpenAPI file in frontmatter (do **not** put remote OpenAPI URLs in frontmatter, and do **not** set tab/group-level `openapi` in `docs.json` navigation—tab `openapi` auto-populates endpoints and duplicates MDX nav entries; remote URLs in frontmatter are not reliably resolved by Mintlify). Specs live under `openapi/application.json` (Application API + webhooks) and `openapi/admin.json` (Admin API), also listed under `api.openapi` in `docs.json`. Forms: `openapi: "/openapi/application.json GET /path"`, `openapi: "/openapi/application.json webhook <eventKey>"`, `openapi: "/openapi/admin.json METHOD /path"`. Keep those lines identical across locales.
- English **nav titles** for those pages come from OpenAPI **`summary`** via `scripts/sync-openapi-titles.mjs` (`npm run translate:sync-openapi-titles`). For target locales the script only fills a missing `title` with English; translate it by hand.
- Mintlify custom heading IDs use markdown `## Title {#slug}`. Slugs come from the English page via `scripts/sync-heading-anchors.mjs` (`npm run translate:sync-anchors`), which keeps explicit English anchors and copies them onto locale headings in document order. Never translate or alter `{#…}`, and keep the same headings, in the same order, in every locale.
- For Mintlify `<Accordion>` components that may be deep-linked, always set explicit `id` values (do not rely on title-derived hashes) and keep those `id`s identical across locales.
- Do not use fragile OpenAPI parameter hash links like `#parameter-*` in docs links; link to the endpoint page and reference parameter names inline in prose/code.
- To add a locale, add a `navigation.languages` block to `docs.json`, mirror every page under `<locale>/`, copy `es/components/` to `<locale>/components/`, and add a section to `i18n/glossary.md`.
